# Line endings are pinned, not left to `core.autocrlf`.
#
# This is not tidiness. Two things in this repo depend on bytes surviving a checkout:
#
# 1. `auditing-artifacts` step 9 records a normalized content hash (`rev`) so a
#    re-audit can short-circuit instead of paying for another full lens fan-out. A
#    hash over content git may translate is stable only until the next clone — and
#    when it moves, the short-circuit stops firing SILENTLY. Nothing errors; every
#    re-audit just quietly costs full price again.
# 2. The audit fixtures under `tests/fixtures/audit/` are graded by substring match
#    against their header contracts, and the reconciler fixture cites exact
#    `file:line` positions. Those are byte-level claims.
#
# On Windows `core.autocrlf` is on by default, so leaving this unspecified means the
# repo behaves differently depending on who cloned it. Everything here is text.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Stated explicitly for the files whose bytes are load-bearing, so the intent
# survives someone relaxing the blanket rule above.
*.md          text eol=lf
*.ts          text eol=lf
*.json        text eol=lf
*.sh          text eol=lf

# Extensionless shell script.
skills/executing-dag-plans/git-commit-safe text eol=lf
